1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,840
Think of leaving the shrine
in Breath of the Wild.

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:06,560
You see a vast landscape,
then step into it yourself.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,560
Admiring scenery is different
from feeling you can go there.

4
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:15,200
Let's explore what to show
players when a game begins.

5
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:20,200
Not menu layouts, but the first
scene the player controls.

6
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:24,780
That first scene promises the
experience this game will offer.

7
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:26,620
Somewhere to explore,

8
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:29,620
or, in a mystery, a clue worth investigating.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,500
But if explanations come before that promise,

10
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,900
you arrive for adventure and
get employee orientation.

11
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:39,620
It's not whether you explain,
but what players experience first.

12
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:44,540
Compare two openings: one explains
the setting and controls first.

13
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:47,940
The other first shows the
hero and a nearby destination.

14
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,920
We're comparing opening
priorities, not features.

15
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:55,040
Either way, players should see
what to expect from this game.

16
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:59,680
Before decorating that first screen,
decide what it should make them feel.

17
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,960
Let's return to the Shrine of Resurrection.

18
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,060
Link wakes, gets the Sheikah Slate,
and opens the way outside.

19
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,060
There are voice cues and
control instructions, too.

20
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:13,000
Breath of the Wild does give you instructions.

21
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:18,920
Notice how it offers something to
try, even in an unfamiliar space.

22
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,020
In the first playable scene,
the character and surroundings

23
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:26,280
must form a readable relationship. Where am I?

24
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:29,160
What looks worth approaching?
Those come first.

25
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:33,140
You can approach an object without
knowing the world's history.

26
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:36,560
Separate understanding the
setting from knowing what to do,

27
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:39,360
and the opening asks less
of players all at once.

28
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:41,880
Keep the hero, the exit,

29
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:46,480
and the object. Change only the
framing: an unclear layout versus

30
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:50,580
one that guides the eye from
the hero to the object and exit.

31
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:53,460
Guidance doesn't just mean adding arrows.

32
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:59,880
Can the framing alone suggest what
to do next? That's a design question.

33
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:06,440
On the Great Plateau, distant terrain
or buildings may catch your interest.

34
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:10,500
Before deciding where to walk,
notice what catches your eye.

35
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:14,760
Breath of the Wild has quest
guidance, too. But beyond that,

36
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:20,280
how does something in the landscape
become a place you want to reach?

37
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:24,220
A wider view doesn't always
make the direction clearer.

38
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:29,500
If everything has equal emphasis,
where should you look first?

39
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,800
Size, contrast, and empty space
can create an order of attention.

40
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:39,320
Rather than reveal the whole route,
choose what first draws the eye.

41
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:42,660
On the same terrain,
compare competing focal points

42
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:46,220
with a view that singles out
one first point of interest.

43
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:49,800
We're not adding destinations
or changing the map.

44
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:53,480
If everything looks important,
the whole screen is raising its hand.

45
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:59,360
Designing that first scene also
means deciding what not to emphasize.

46
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:04,030
Watch how you notice and pick
up a nearby item on the Plateau.

47
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:09,810
Even without a big battle, looking around
and finding something is an experience.

48
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:14,990
Focus less on the item mechanic and
more on its role in the opening.

49
00:03:15,350 --> 00:03:18,330
It suggests a world that
invites you to look around.

50
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:22,890
The first action isn't just a controls
check. Is this a game about observing,

51
00:03:22,890 --> 00:03:27,510
avoiding danger, or making choices?
The player can learn that by doing.

52
00:03:27,510 --> 00:03:31,890
Demand too many actions at once,
and what players learned may become unclear.

53
00:03:32,270 --> 00:03:36,510
First choose the one action you
want them to experience here.

54
00:03:36,650 --> 00:03:40,750
In the same space, compare many
instructions appearing at once

55
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:43,850
with inviting players to
inspect one nearby object first.

56
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:47,270
This shows how the scope of
their first attempt changes.

57
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:51,370
A simple beginning doesn't mean
the whole game must be simple.

58
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:55,070
Does a deep game need to throw
players straight into the deep end?

59
00:03:55,070 --> 00:03:57,170
Think about how you ease them in.

60
00:03:57,950 --> 00:04:01,930
In the Magnesis shrine,
moving metal plates opens a path.

61
00:04:02,110 --> 00:04:04,190
It doesn't just announce a new ability.

62
00:04:04,190 --> 00:04:07,330
You see what changes when you use it.

63
00:04:07,870 --> 00:04:10,970
Don't just watch the object moving.

64
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:15,190
Notice how that movement
connects to the route ahead.

65
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:19,210
After an interaction, players
need a sign that it worked.

66
00:04:19,470 --> 00:04:25,390
If nothing seems to change, they may press
the button again instead of moving on.

67
00:04:25,530 --> 00:04:27,930
Good feedback isn't just a flash of light.

68
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:30,990
The link between your action and
the world's change should be clear,

69
00:04:30,990 --> 00:04:33,710
and that change should give
you a reason to continue.

70
00:04:34,210 --> 00:04:39,970
Keep the button and door. Compare
an off-screen result with a view

71
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:45,450
that shows the door opening. It's about
readable results, not switch code.

72
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:50,270
If the result must be off-screen,
sound or a brief camera cue can help.

73
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:52,790
One frame need not show everything.

74
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:55,850
The key is a clear connection
between cause and effect.

75
00:04:56,950 --> 00:05:01,510
As you explore the Great Plateau,
new problems like cold appear.

76
00:05:01,630 --> 00:05:06,670
You check temperature and status,
then consider clothing or food effects.

77
00:05:06,810 --> 00:05:11,830
This comes later than the first
playable scene. The learning begun there

78
00:05:11,830 --> 00:05:16,450
develops into another experience
when players meet a new danger.

79
00:05:16,590 --> 00:05:22,570
An opening needn't be safe. Some games
need a dangerous or unsettling start.

80
00:05:22,690 --> 00:05:26,310
But what players understand
after failing matters.

81
00:05:26,310 --> 00:05:29,230
Recognizing a failure's cause
and changing your approach

82
00:05:29,230 --> 00:05:33,230
isn't the same as being hurt for no
clear reason. Whatever the difficulty,

83
00:05:33,230 --> 00:05:36,210
check the visual clues
that explain the danger.

84
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:39,010
With the same hazard,
one version hides its cause;

85
00:05:39,010 --> 00:05:43,270
the other shows how the danger zone
relates to the change in status.

86
00:05:43,410 --> 00:05:47,130
We're not removing difficulty,
but making its cause readable.

87
00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:52,350
If getting back to another attempt takes
too long, early learning becomes a chore.

88
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:56,690
Design both the danger cues and
the path back after failure.

89
00:05:57,450 --> 00:05:59,750
The Old Man offers guidance and dialogue,

90
00:05:59,750 --> 00:06:02,550
and there's a goal for
leaving the Great Plateau.

91
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:07,710
Normally, you finish four shrines, get the
paraglider, and leave for the next area.

92
00:06:08,070 --> 00:06:13,090
So calling this an opening with no
explanations or restrictions misses the point.

93
00:06:13,650 --> 00:06:16,310
Guidance and exploration exist together.

94
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:18,510
When choosing opening information,

95
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:22,950
separate what's needed to act
now from what's needed later.

96
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:28,070
It may not be wrong information, just too
much arriving before there's a use for it.

97
00:06:28,410 --> 00:06:32,090
Some scenes need an explanation
just to make the goal clear.

98
00:06:32,690 --> 00:06:34,430
Don't just cut instructions.

99
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:37,910
Ask whether they answer the
player's question right now.

100
00:06:38,310 --> 00:06:41,910
Keep the information the same,
and change only when it's given.

101
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:44,270
Compare showing it all at the start

102
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:47,830
with giving relevant guidance
after the first action.

103
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:51,670
In a story-driven game, opening
dialogue may be the core experience.

104
00:06:51,785 --> 00:06:54,370
It's not bad cutscenes
versus good interaction.

105
00:06:54,370 --> 00:06:58,710
Ask whether what you're showing
starts this game's experience.

106
00:06:59,450 --> 00:07:05,050
Gliding off the Plateau lets you explore
the world you've seen in a new way.

107
00:07:05,370 --> 00:07:08,610
The opening's actions carry
into the adventure ahead.

108
00:07:09,030 --> 00:07:12,930
Don't judge an opening only by
how good its first screen looks.

109
00:07:13,310 --> 00:07:17,650
Does the expectation it creates
carry into the next scene?

110
00:07:17,770 --> 00:07:22,830
Ask three questions about your first
scene: what should players see first?

111
00:07:22,830 --> 00:07:27,590
What should they try? Why should the
result make them want to continue?

112
00:07:27,590 --> 00:07:31,370
The advice about the first three
minutes isn't a timer for every game.

113
00:07:31,370 --> 00:07:35,350
Treat it as a question:
when does the core experience begin?

114
00:07:35,530 --> 00:07:38,130
Rushing the presentation isn't the point.

115
00:07:38,570 --> 00:07:41,770
Finally, compare a start that
stalls after its first goal

116
00:07:41,770 --> 00:07:44,730
with one whose result reveals
a new point of interest.

117
00:07:45,190 --> 00:07:50,330
Where a scene ends and what it leaves
behind can change the reason to act next.

118
00:07:50,750 --> 00:07:53,670
A game's first scene isn't a
manual explaining everything.

119
00:07:53,670 --> 00:07:58,170
It's an invitation to try. Rather than
list everything you want to teach,

120
00:07:58,170 --> 00:08:01,030
design the experience you're
inviting players into.
